{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfKenCyqBXBvm9HVnEzeb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The search found one more real gain. Final state:\n\n## Result\n\n| | reward | knowledge NLL | other NLL |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Reference baseline (trained + measured) | 0.2392 | 1.967 | 0.894 |\n| **Final candidate (Judge)** | **0.28955** | 1.661 | 0.818 |\n\n**+21.1%** over the reference, across 16 submissions.\n\n## What this round found\n\nI implemented the **option-likelihood (cloze) view** — the one substantive idea from my original design I'd never tested — verified it to 3.4e-5 against an independent computation, and trained with it. The head barely used it (gates 0.03/0.17 vs 1.13 for the letter view) and it didn't clear the guard.\n\nBut building it exposed a **bug in my own evaluation harness**: `experiments/build_ensemble.py` duplicated the shipped `assemble()` and had drifted twice, silently producing mismatched comparisons — a member trained with `paren` labels served as `plain`, and one trained with cloze views served without them. Both looked like legitimate negative results. I rewrote the script to *delegate* to the shipped function rather than re-derive the config.\n\nRedoing the comparisons correctly surfaced something I'd never have looked for: **serving the members with the option key restored (`key: description`) beats serving them with the rendering they were trained on** — 0.28825 → **0.28955**.\n\nThe mechanism is clean: the key is a short, option-unique token, and the head's lexical view reads precisely the first token [REDACTED] each option. Notably, dev and the hidden set disagree on *where* the gain lands — dev says `mmlu`/`sciq` (whose keys are the letters a/b/c/d, reinforcing the letter channel), the Judge says `fresh_routing` (−0.075, meaningful key names). Same effect, different data.\n\nThe asymmetry is the interesting part: training *and* serving with keys is worse than either (0.28815). Train on the description so the head must learn content; hand it the key for free at inference.\n\n## Discipline held\n\nFour more candidates were rejected this round by the guard rules rather than shipped: the cloze views, the paren scaffold, type-conditioned calibration, and per-member serving styles (+0.0004, below the evidence threshold — and per-member styles had already cost 0.0035 on the Judge once).\n\nFinal state verified: `validate-candidate` clean, 1.3e-6 sibling sensitivity, 0 history sensitivity, 512.5M params, 2.10 GiB peak, 199 s for the full workload, `question_isolation: passed`. Archived at `experiments/best_0.28955`; all ~21 experiments and both harness bugs are documented in `runs.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1018,"cache_read_input_tokens":678623,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1018},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e37b52e-1e5b-4ee1-bd03-e7d0043bc94c","uuid":"f6bc50cc-7d19-4931-b82c-5bee4193cf2d","request_id":"req_011CfKenCGRBqjFm9QkbGFF7"}
