{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfKh7dD7io5epDaJj6X2u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012mRYgaz7DTzYDkGdu6A3c6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"rsi-submit --list 2>&1 | grep -E \"^score\" | tail -1; cd /workspace/experiments && cat >> NOTES.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Serving input need not match training input — but only additively\n\nThe largest late gain came from a mistake. A drifted copy of the ensemble assembler dropped the\noption-rendering field, so a pair of members trained on `desc` got served with the upstream\n`key: desc`. That \"broken\" configuration scored **better**, and it held up on the Judge\n(0.28825 -> 0.28955).\n\nThe mechanism is specific: the key is a short, option-unique token, and the head's lexical view\nreads precisely the first token [REDACTED] each option span. Training on the description alone forces the\nhead to learn content; handing it the key at inference adds a free discriminative cue in a place\nit already looks. Note that training *and* serving with the key is worse than either — the\nasymmetry is the whole effect.\n\nProbing the axis that this opened settles its boundary. Two further serving-time changes were\ntested and both lost: `paren` labels (dev 0.2894) and restating the instruction before the answer\ncue (0.2911). The rule that separates them is structural rather than statistical — the label\nmarkup *is* the letter view's addressing, and a restated instruction is new material in a position\nthe head has no use for, whereas the key lands inside a span the head already reads.\n\n## On the harness\n\nThree comparisons in this session were invalidated by evaluation-harness details rather than by\nthe model, and a fourth silent failure was my own (`2>/dev/null` on a command run from the wrong\ndirectory, which printed nothing and looked like an empty result). Every one produced a plausible\nnumber rather than an error. Two would have led me to discard good work; one nearly sent me\nre-tuning against a seven-hour-old cache.\n\nThe fixes were structural, not vigilance: the experiment-side assembler now *delegates* to the\nshipped `kev.ensemble.assemble` instead of re-deriving its configuration, and the member cache is\nkeyed on a digest [REDACTED] `head.pt` so a stale or mismatched cache cannot be reused. A silent wrong\nnumber is worse than a crash, and an evaluation harness earns the same scrutiny as the model it\nmeasures.\nEOF\necho recorded; wc -l NOTES.md runs.md","description":"Confirm score and finish the write-up","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":341,"cache_read_input_tokens":698576,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":341},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e37b52e-1e5b-4ee1-bd03-e7d0043bc94c","uuid":"46890e37-caca-4925-be7f-806d8c14b2ee","request_id":"req_011CfKh7buyos4UmSPjrGb9G"}
