{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfKiKsYmgSrUEyTdPJP5N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ENfRBH9wM63HrueuA8fs4k","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"rsi-submit --list 2>&1 | grep -E \"^score\" | tail -1; cd /workspace/experiments && cat >> NOTES.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Where the serving-enrichment rule stops\n\nThe rule that came out of the option-key win — *additive content inside a span the head already\nreads helps; structural change hurts* — needed one more qualifier, found by testing a defect that\nlooked eminently fixable.\n\nThe head's lexical view reads each option's first token. Ordinal level descriptions routinely\nshare one (\"very negative\"/\"very positive\"), and **94% of the fixed corpus's ordinal questions\ncollide**, so that view is blind on them. Prefixing the level index makes every first token\nunique — additive content, in exactly the span the head reads. It changed nothing (dev 0.2923\nagainst 0.2924).\n\nThe reason is that the head *learned* the lexical view is useless for ordinal questions and routes\naround it, and the view gates are shared across question types, so nothing makes it trusted again\nat inference. Enrichment pays only where the head already **relies** on the channel being\nenriched. Fixing this properly means training with indexed levels; that is written up as the\nclearest remaining lead rather than attempted, because the evidence does not justify another\nretrain on its own.\nEOF\necho recorded; grep -c \"^| \" runs.md","description":"Confirm the score and finish the write-up","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":412,"cache_read_input_tokens":709036,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":412},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnos